/**
\page jmap JMAP map file format

This page describes the JMAP file format, which is a JSON based file format used to describe the register map of numeric-addressed devices. It has been introduced to replace the classic plain-text map file format and to provide additional features which would not fit into the old format. The numeric-addressed backends (e.g. PCIe, XDMA, UIO, and the Dummy backend) can be told to use a JMAP file by passing the \c map parameter in the ChimeraTK device descriptor:
\verbatim
(pci:pciedevs5?map=mydevice.jmap)
(dummy?map=mydevice.jmap)
\endverbatim

Commonly the register maps are generated by firmware tools like the FWK firmware framework, but they can just as well be written by hand in case no such generation tool exists.

\section jmap_fundamentals Fundamental concepts

A JMAP file is a single JSON object with the following top-level keys:
\li \c mapFormatVersion - a version string of the file format (currently \c "0.0.1").
\li \c metadata - an object holding arbitrary key-value pairs describing the firmware (see \ref jmap_metadata).
\li \c interruptHandler - a description of the interrupt controllers (see \ref jmap_interrupts).
\li \c addressSpace - the list of registers and modules making up the register map.

The heart of the file is the \c addressSpace array. Each entry in it is either a register, or a \e module which groups other registers and modules below it. Registers are addressed by a hierarchical path, which is built from the \c name entries of the nested objects (modules and registers), separated by \c '/' or \c '.', e.g. \c APP/STATUS or \c APP.STATUS (\c '/' and \c '.' have synonymous meaning and can be exchanged arbitrarily). This path is used to access the register through register accessors, as described in \ref accessor1d and \ref accessor2d.

\subsection jmap_comments Comments

JSON does not support comments natively. By convention, entries whose key starts with an underscore are treated as comments and ignored by the parser:
\verbatim
{
  "_": "This is a comment and is ignored",
  "_comment": "This is another comment and is ignored as well",
  "mapFormatVersion": "0.0.1",
  "metadata": {},
  "interruptHandler": {},
  "addressSpace": []
}
\endverbatim

Any unknown key not starting with an underscore is reserved for future use in later map format versions and hence should not be used as comments.

\section jmap_register Register entry

A register is an entry carrying an \c address (or inheriting one from a parent module, see \ref jmap_modules). This section describes the individual members of a register entry.

\subsection jmap_address Address

The minimal register entry needs only a \c name and an \c address:
\verbatim
{
  "name": "SomeTopLevelRegister",
  "address": { "type": "IO", "channel": 0, "offset": 32 }
}
\endverbatim

The \c address object has the following members:
\li \c type - either \c "IO" (the default, an address on the I/O bus) or \c "DMA". A DMA address is typically used for bulk data acquisition registers.
\li \c channel - the bus channel (bar) number, defaults to \c 0. For DMA addresses the channel is mapped to a device bar by adding an offset of 13, mirroring the convention of the old map file format in which DMA bars started at an offset of 13 (so a DMA channel of \c 0 corresponds to device bar 13).
\li \c offset - the byte offset of the register inside the channel, given either as a plain decimal number or as a hexadecimal string.

An \c address may also be omitted and inherited from a parent module; this is described together with modules in \ref jmap_modules.

\subsection jmap_access Access

The \c access member defines how a register may be accessed:
\li \c "RW" - read/write access (the default).
\li \c "RO" - read-only access, e.g. for status registers.
\li \c "WO" - write-only access, values cannot be read back.

Example:
\verbatim
{
  "name": "STATUS",
  "access": "RO",
  "address": { "channel": 2, "offset": "0x8000" }
}
\endverbatim

\subsection jmap_elements Number of elements and bytes per element

By default a register holds a single element of 4 bytes. A register holding more than one element is declared by giving the number of elements and the size of each element:
\li \c numberOfElements - the number of elements, defaults to \c 1.
\li \c bytesPerElement - the size of a single element in bytes, defaults to \c 4.

Example:
\verbatim
{
  "name": "SomeTable",
  "access": "WO",
  "numberOfElements": 16384,
  "bytesPerElement": 2,
  "address": { "channel": 0, "offset": 2048 }
}
\endverbatim

\subsection jmap_representation Representation

The \c representation object describes how the raw data is interpreted by the application layer. The following members and values are supported:
\li \c type - one of \c "fixedPoint" (the default), \c "IEEE754" (single or double precision floating point, see below), \c "string" (an ASCII string) or \c "void" (used for pure interrupt sources, see \ref jmap_interrupts).
\li \c width - the width of the value in bits. For \c fixedPoint it may be smaller than the element size, in which case the remaining bits are ignored. Default: \c 32
\li \c fractionalBits - the number of fractional bits of a \c fixedPoint value; may be negative to describe integer values with a scaling factor. Must be 0 unless type is \c fixedPoint. Default: \c 0
\li \c isSigned - whether the value is interpreted as signed. Must be \c true for IEEE754 types. Default: \c false
\li \c bitShift - the bit offset of the value within the element. Default: \c 0

If the entire representation is omitted, it defaults to a 32-bit unsigned integer.

A single-precision floating point register:
\verbatim
{
  "name": "ExampleRegister",
  "address": { "channel": 0, "offset": 32 },
  "representation": {
    "type": "fixedPoint",
    "width": 26,
    "fractionalBits": 12,
    "isSigned": true
  }
}
\endverbatim

\subsection jmap_description Description and engineering unit

\li \c description - a free-form human-readable text describing the purpose of the register.
\li \c engineeringUnit - the physical unit of the register value, e.g. \c "mV".

Both are purely informational and may be omitted. Example:
\verbatim
{
  "name": "SomeTopLevelRegister",
  "engineeringUnit": "mV",
  "description": "This is an example register",
  "address": { "channel": 0, "offset": 32 }
}
\endverbatim

\section jmap_modules Modules and hierarchical names

\subsection jmap_simple_modules Modules

An entry in the \c addressSpace without an \c address is a module: it groups nested registers and modules below it by providing a \c children list, and contributes its \c name to the hierarchical path of everything beneath it. A module is not a register and carries no data of its own. Example (from \c simpleJsonFile.jmap):
\verbatim
{
  "name": "APP",
  "children": [
    {
      "name": "STATUS",
      "access": "RO",
      "address": { "type": "IO", "channel": 2, "offset": "0x8000" }
    },
    {
      "name": "CONTROL",
      "access": "RW",
      "address": { "type": "IO", "channel": 2, "offset": "0x8004" }
    }
  ]
}
\endverbatim
This yields the registers \c APP.STATUS and \c APP.CONTROL.

Note: a module simply becomes a register by adding an address. This can be used to describe a hierarchical address space by describing the raw address space e.g. for a firmware module at the higher level of the hierarchy, while adding the detailed registers in the firmware module's address space as children. Keep in mind that there is no technical necessity that all children have to have addresses inside the address space of their parents (although it is certainly a good convention). Example:
\verbatim
{
  "name": "APP",
  "address": { "type": "IO", "channel": 2, "offset": "0x8000" },
  "numberOfElements": 2,
  "children": [
    {
      "name": "STATUS",
      "access": "RO",
      "address": { "type": "IO", "channel": 2, "offset": "0x8000" },
      "representation": { "width": 3 }
    },
    {
      "name": "CONTROL",
      "access": "RW",
      "address": { "type": "IO", "channel": 2, "offset": "0x8004" },
      "representation": { "width": 5 }
    }
  ]
}
\endverbatim

This yields the same registers \c APP/STATUS and \c APP.CONTROL as before, but in addition a register \c APP for the raw area containing both registers.


\subsection jmap_address_inheritance Address inheritance

A child entry does not have to carry an address of its own: it inherits the address of the nearest ancestor that does. The inheritance works per field of the address entry, so e.g. only the \c type and \c channel may be inherited while the \c offset is overridden. Example (identical outcome to the last example above):
\verbatim
{
  "name": "APP",
  "address": { "type": "IO", "channel": 2, "offset": "0x8000" },
  "numberOfElements": 2,
  "children": [
    {
      "name": "STATUS",
      "access": "RO",
      "representation": { "width": 3 }
    },
    {
      "name": "CONTROL",
      "access": "RW",
      "address": { "offset": "0x8004" },
      "representation": { "width": 5 }
    }
  ]
}
\endverbatim

\section jmap_advanced Advanced features

\subsection jmap_interrupts Interrupts

The \c interruptHandler top-level object describes interrupt controllers which are used to multiplex multiple interrupt sources in firmware into the same hardware interrupt request. Each key is an interrupt id and each entry carries an \c INTC controller with its configuration and optionally nested \c subhandler entries:
\verbatim
"interruptHandler": {
  "3": {
    "INTC": { "path": "MY_INTC", "options": ["MER"], "version": 1 },
    "subhandler": {
      "0": {
        "INTC": { "path": "MY_INTC.SUB0", "options": [] }
      }
    }
  }
}
\endverbatim

Registers can be triggered by an interrupt. The \c "triggeredByInterrupt" field contains the full interrupt id, which is an array of all ids and sub-ids as described in the \c interruptHandler object. Such registers are always read-only, hence the \c "triggeredByInterrupt" field is mutually exclusive with the \c "access" field.

Plain interrupts have a \c representation of type \c "void" and must not have an address. Example:
\verbatim
{
  "name": "PRIMARY_INTERRUPT_3",
  "triggeredByInterrupt": [3],
  "representation": { "type": "void" }
},
{
  "name": "SUB_INTERRUPT_3_0_2",
  "triggeredByInterrupt": [3,0,2],
  "representation": { "type": "void" }
}
\endverbatim

\c PRIMARY_INTERRUPT_3 describes the primary interrupt (IRQ) 3, while \c SUB_INTERRUPT_3_0_2 will be the interrupt number 2 of the subhandler 0 inside the handler for the primary interrupt 3 (i.e. interrupt \c 3:0:2).

When interrupts shall be used to trigger data read-out, they can be added to normal registers:
\verbatim
{
  "name": "TRIGGERED_DATA",
  "address": { "offset": "0x8100" },
  "numberOfElements": 2048,
  "triggeredByInterrupt": [3,0,2],
  "representation": { "width": 24, "fractionalBits": 10, "isSigned": true }
}
\endverbatim
This will create a push-type register (supporting ChimeraTK::AccessMode::wait_for_new_data) that will receive new values when the interrupt \c 3:0:2 arrives by polling the data from the specified address. See \ref design_AsyncNDRegisterAccessor_NumericAddressed for more details.


\subsection jmap_2d 2D multiplexed registers

A register holding several channels of time-multiplexed samples is declared by giving a \c channelTabs entry:
\li \c numberOfElements - the number of samples (time slots) per channel.
\li \c pitch - the number of bytes between two samples of the same channel.
\li \c channels - the list of channels of this tab. Each channel has a \c name, a byte \c offset relative to the register base address, a \c bytesPerElement and an optional \c representation.

Channels can have arbitrary names; single-word channels are simply indexed \c "0", \c "1", ... Example:
\verbatim
{
  "name": "DMA",
  "address": { "type": "DMA", "offset": "0x0" },
  "channelTabs": [
    {
      "numberOfElements": 16384,
      "pitch": 32,
      "channels": [
        {
          "name": "0",
          "offset": 0,
          "bytesPerElement": 2,
          "representation": { "type": "fixedPoint", "width": 16 }
        },
        {
          "name": "1",
          "offset": 2,
          "bytesPerElement": 2,
          "representation": { "type": "fixedPoint", "width": 12, "fractionalBits": 12 }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
\endverbatim
Here \c DMA is a 2D register with two channels, each holding 16384 samples, with a pitch of 32 bytes between the samples of a channel (so there is space for more channels in this example).

\subsubsection jmap_channel_slices Channel slices

For every channel, a read-only one-dimensional register slice is created automatically. The slice is named after the channel, e.g. \c DMA/0, \c DMA/1 in the example above. Each slice exposes the samples of that single channel as a one-dimensional array, reading them out of the 2D register with the correct stride. The channel's byte \c offset is folded into the slice address, so it can be addressed independently of the parent 2D register. Writing is deliberately not supported for these slices, since writing a single channel of a 2D register would require a read-modify-write cycle across the channels. If multiple slice accessors for the same 2D register shall be read simultaneously (without ChimeraTK::AccessMode::wait_for_new_data), it is highly recommended to add them all to the same ChimeraTK::TransferGroup to avoid transferring the full 2D data multiple times unnecessarily from the device.

\subsubsection jmap_channel_tabs Channel tabs

The example above uses a single channel tab, which is the common case. For multi-tab DAQ setups an additional \c tabSelectRegister can be used to switch between the tabs, but the support for multiple tabs is not yet implemented.

\subsection jmap_double_buffering Double buffering

A register that is fed by double-buffered DMA hardware is marked with a \c doubleBuffering object. It contains:
\li \c secondaryBufferAddress - the address of the second (inactive) buffer, with the same members as a regular \c address.
\li \c enableRegister - path of the register used to enable double buffering.
\li \c readBufferRegister - path of the register reporting which buffer is currently active.
\li \c index - the index of the buffer pair within the control registers.

In addition to the main register, two read-only registers named \c NAME/BUF0 and \c NAME/BUF1 are created for the two buffers. Example:
\verbatim
{
  "name": "CTRL",
  "triggeredByInterrupt": [3, 0, 1],
  "address": { "type": "DMA", "offset": "0x40000" },
  "doubleBuffering": {
    "secondaryBufferAddress": { "type": "DMA", "offset": "0x40200" },
    "enableRegister": "DAQ.DOUBLE_BUF.ENA",
    "readBufferRegister": "DAQ.DOUBLE_BUF.INACTIVE_BUF_ID",
    "index": 0
  },
  "channelTabs": [
    {
      "numberOfElements": 16384,
      "pitch": 64,
      "channels": [
        {
          "name": "errorI",
          "bytesPerElement": 2,
          "offset": 0,
          "representation": {
            "type": "fixedPoint",
            "width": 16,
            "fractionalBits": -2,
            "isSigned": true
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
\endverbatim

\subsection jmap_bitfields Bit fields

A register can carry a list of \c children which are bit fields of the register word. Each child has a \c name and a \c representation with \c bitShift and \c width; the children share the starting address of their parent register (see \ref jmap_address_inheritance). The full word register itself is also accessible. Example:
\verbatim
{
  "name": "STATUS",
  "access": "RO",
  "address": { "type": "IO", "channel": 2, "offset": "0x8000" },
  "bytesPerElement": 4,
  "children": [
    {
      "name": "ProbeLimiter",
      "representation": { "width": 1 }
    },
    {
      "name": "ExternalInterlock",
      "representation": { "bitShift": 1, "width": 1 }
    },
    {
      "name": "ErrorCounter",
      "representation": {
        "bitShift": 2,
        "type": "fixedPoint",
        "width": 3,
        "fractionalBits": 0,
        "isSigned": false
      }
    }
  ]
}
\endverbatim
Registers which share the starting address with a bit range and have a width smaller than their element size are automatically recognised as bit ranges as well.

\section jmap_metadata Metadata

The \c metadata object can carry any number of key-value pairs describing the firmware. Keys are required to be non-empty and must not start with an underscore. All entries are collected into the metadata catalogue and can be queried by application code. Example:
\verbatim
"metadata": {
  "mapfileRevision": "1.8.3-0-gdeadbeef",
  "someRandomEntry": "some random value"
}
\endverbatim

\section jmap_references See also

\li \ref dmap
\li \ref numeric_addresses
\li \ref accessor1d
\li \ref accessor2d
*/
